From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toon Verwaest <verwaest@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 09:14:13 +0200
Subject: [parser] Restrict arrow cover-grammar extensions to arrow head scopes

`ParseExpressionCoverGrammar` is used both for parenthesized
`CoverParenthesizedExpressionAndArrowParameterList` (in an
`ArrowHeadParsingScope`) and for ordinary `Expression`s (in an
`ExpressionParsingScope`).

Previously, `ParseExpressionCoverGrammar` unconditionally checked for
`...` (`ParseArrowParametersWithRest`) and trailing commas before `) =>`
regardless of the current `ExpressionScope`. As a result, non-arrow
expressions such as `a, ...x = 1` or `a, ...x, y` reported arrow rest
parameter errors instead of `Unexpected token '...'`, and `if (a,) => {}`
accepted the trailing comma in the `if` condition and reported
`Unexpected token '=>'` instead of `Unexpected token ')'`.

Additionally, `ParseBindingPattern` eagerly reported
`kStrictEvalArguments` when parsing an identifier in strict mode instead
of deferring to `ClassifyParameter` / `RecordStrictModeParameterError`.
In `"use strict"; ({a = 42}, ...eval) => {}`, this caused
`ParseBindingPattern` to abort before `=>`, so `ParsePrimaryExpression`
fell back to `ValidateExpression()` and reported `Invalid shorthand
property initializer` at `{a = 42}`, and in `"use strict"; (1, ...eval) => {}`
it reported `eval` instead of the earlier invalid parameter `1`.

Guard rest parameters and trailing commas in
`ParseExpressionCoverGrammar` with `CanBeArrowParameterDeclaration()`,
and let `ClassifyParameter` handle strict `eval`/`arguments` in
`ParseBindingPattern`.

